WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.003
A payments store spreads its keys over four nodes.

2
00:00:06.003 --> 00:00:12.676
Each key is hashed to a number, and its node is that number mod 4: the remainder after dividing by 4.

3
00:00:12.867 --> 00:00:15.560
Take the key that hashes to 37.

4
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.775
37 mod 4 is 1, so it lives on node 1.

5
00:00:18.775 --> 00:00:21.121
Every node holds five keys.

6
00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:24.261
Before a sales peak, a fifth node joins.

7
00:00:24.261 --> 00:00:26.357
The rule becomes hash mod 5.

8
00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:30.572
Almost every answer changes: 16 of the 20 keys move.

9
00:00:30.572 --> 00:00:35.464
Key 37 goes from node 1 to node 2, and neither of those is new.

10
00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:38.882
The new node needed only four, its fair share.

11
00:00:38.882 --> 00:00:41.213
The other twelve moves are waste.

12
00:00:41.213 --> 00:00:44.251
From ten nodes to eleven, 91% of keys move.

13
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.080
So split the map in two.

14
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:53.011
First, hash mod 20 puts each key in one of 20 partitions, a number fixed when the store is created.

15
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.916
Then a table says which node holds each partition: five each.

16
00:00:57.916 --> 00:01:01.704
Key 37 is in partition 17, which lives on node 1.

17
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:03.564
Now the fifth node joins.

18
00:01:03.564 --> 00:01:08.953
Its fair share is four partitions, so each old node hands it one whole partition.

19
00:01:09.833 --> 00:01:19.675
No key changes partition, so only the keys in those four partitions move: one in five, all to the new node, and four old nodes share the copying.

20
00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:25.569
A client still holds the old table, so it sends a request for key 37 to node 1.

21
00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:28.750
Node 1 no longer owns partition 17.

22
00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:33.097
It answers MOVED: partition 17 lives on node 4 now.

23
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.686
The client updates its table and retries at node 4.

24
00:01:36.686 --> 00:01:40.204
The request costs one extra hop, and it doesn't fail.

25
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.175
Fix the partitions once and move them whole.

26
00:01:43.175 --> 00:01:49.672
A new node takes only its share, and a client with an old map is one redirect away from the right node.
